---
<p>This page lists every kind of type in Bayan, what it holds and the rules that come with it. Each section has a small program, and where the compiler rejects a misuse, the diagnostic code and its real message. Features the compiler does not have yet are marked as planned and shown as fragments only.</p>

<h2 id="primitives">Primitive types</h2>
<div class="tablewrap"><table>
  <thead><tr><th scope="col">Type</th><th scope="col">What it is</th></tr></thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td><code>i8 i16 i32 i64</code></td><td>Signed integers of 8, 16, 32 and 64 bits.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>u8 u16 u32 u64</code></td><td>Unsigned integers.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>isize usize</code></td><td>Signed and unsigned integers the width of a pointer.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>f32 f64</code></td><td>Floating-point numbers.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>bool</code></td><td><code>true</code> or <code>false</code>.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>rune</code></td><td>One Unicode scalar value, 32 bits wide.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>string</code></td><td>Valid UTF-8 text. See <a href="#strings">Strings</a>.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>never</code></td><td>The type of an expression that does not return. See <a href="#never">never</a>.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>dec</code></td><td>A decimal type for exact decimal arithmetic.</td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table></div>
<p>A project may spell the integer and float types the C# way (<code>int</code>, <code>long</code>, <code>double</code>) by setting <code>style.primitives</code>. The two spellings mean the same type, and <code>bayan format</code> rewrites a project to its own. <code>char</code>, <code>decimal</code> and <code>object</code> have no equivalent.</p>
<note>Planned: <code>dec</code> has no literal yet (<code>1.50dec</code> is rejected with BYN2022), and the <code>style.primitives</code> setting is read from <code>package.byn</code>, which is covered in the command reference.</note>

<h2 id="numbers">Number conversions and arithmetic</h2>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Widening is implicit</strong> when no value can be lost: an <code>i8</code> goes where an <code>i64</code> is expected.</li>
  <li><strong>Narrowing is written as a call</strong>: <code>u8(x)</code>. It stops the program with a panic in every build mode if the value does not fit.</li>
  <li><code>u8.tryFrom(x)</code> gives <code>u8?</code>, which is <code>null</code> when the value does not fit.</li>
  <li><code>+%</code>, <code>-%</code> and <code>*%</code> wrap around at the type's size. <code>+|</code>, <code>-|</code> and <code>*|</code> stop at the largest or smallest value.</li>
  <li>Plain <code>+</code>, <code>-</code> and <code>*</code> stop the program on overflow, in every build mode. Division by zero does too.</li>
  <li>Integer division rounds toward zero. Converting a float to an integer drops the fraction.</li>
</ul>
<example name="ref-types-primitives"/>
<note>Planned: <code>x.truncate&lt;u8&gt;()</code>, <code>bitcast&lt;T&gt;(x)</code> and <code>checked(expr)</code> are in the specification but not in the compiler yet.</note>

<h2 id="strings">Strings</h2>
<p>A <code>string</code> is always valid UTF-8 and cannot be changed. It is a view of bytes: a literal, a slice of another string, or text it owns.</p>
<ul>
  <li><code>s.length</code> is the number of bytes and costs nothing. <code>s.runeCount</code> counts runes and reads the whole string.</li>
  <li>Every offset is a byte offset. <code>s[a..b]</code> slices by bytes and stops the program with a panic if an end falls inside a character.</li>
  <li>A string has no <code>s[i]</code>. Read a byte with <code>s.bytes[i]</code>, a slice with <code>s[a..b]</code>, or take characters with <code>s.runes.take(n)</code> (BYN2201).</li>
  <li>Searching returns byte positions or <code>bool</code>: <code>contains</code>, <code>startsWith</code>, <code>endsWith</code>, <code>position</code> (an <code>i64?</code>).</li>
  <li><code>parts(by:)</code> and <code>lines</code> split lazily and return views. Nothing is copied.</li>
  <li>Members that return new text, such as <code>lowercased()</code>, <code>uppercased()</code> and <code>replaced(_, by:)</code>, allocate.</li>
  <li><code>s.copy()</code> makes an independent copy, which is how a borrowed string outlives its source.</li>
  <li><code>==</code> compares the bytes. There is no ambient locale.</li>
  <li>There is no <code>+</code> on strings (BYN2209). Join with <code>$"{a}{b}"</code>.</li>
</ul>
<example name="ref-types-strings"/>
<pre><code class="byn">let s = "abc"
print($"{s[1]}")</code></pre>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN2201:</span> a `string` cannot be indexed
  --&gt; str-index.byn:2:11

<span class="gut">  2 │</span> print($"{s[1]}")
<span class="gut">    │</span>           <span class="hl">^^^ strings have no indexer</span>
<span class="gut">    │</span>
  help: read a byte, slice, or take characters; `string` is UTF-8, so position 1 is not a character:
          `s.bytes[1]`       the byte at offset 1 (applied below)
          `s[a..b]`          the text between byte offsets a and b
          `s.runes.take(n)`  the first n runes
          `s.graphemes`      user-perceived characters
<span class="gut">    │</span> 2 │ print($"{s.bytes[1]}")
<span class="gut">    │</span>   │           ++++++</pre>
  <figcaption>The compiler’s message for this program.</figcaption>
</figure>

<h2 id="symbols">Symbols</h2>
<p><code>Symbol</code> is an interned name: a 4-byte value that compares by identity in constant time and orders by its text. A string literal where a <code>Symbol</code> is expected becomes one when the program is built. Any other string needs <code>Symbol.intern(text)</code>, which allocates and keeps the text until the program ends (BYN2832). <code>sym.text</code> is the string back, and <code>$"{sym}"</code> writes it. There is no conversion to or from an integer.</p>
<example name="ref-types-symbol"/>

<h2 id="units">Units and distinct types</h2>
<p>A distinct type wraps one existing type so that values of different meanings cannot be mixed. <code>type distinct UserId = i64</code> makes a type that does not convert to or from <code>i64</code> by itself. Build one by calling it, <code>UserId(7)</code>. Read the wrapped value with <code>.value</code>. A bare literal never becomes a distinct type: <code>let id: UserId = 0</code> is BYN3204, and the fix is <code>UserId(0)</code>. Two distinct types compare only with the same type.</p>
<p>A distinct type can carry a <code>requires</code> rule. Then building one can fail, which is covered in <a href="ref-statements.html#requires">Statements</a>.</p>
<example name="ref-types-distinct"/>
<pre><code class="byn">type distinct Qty = i32 requires it &gt; 0

let q: Qty = 0</code></pre>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN3204:</span> `0` is not a `Qty`
  --&gt; union-lit.byn:3:14

<span class="gut">  3 │</span> let q: Qty = 0
<span class="gut">    │</span>              <span class="hl">^</span>
<span class="gut">    │</span>
  help: wrap it: `Qty(0)`
<span class="gut">    │</span> 3 │ let q: Qty = Qty(0)
<span class="gut">    │</span>   │              ++++++</pre>
  <figcaption>The compiler’s message for this program.</figcaption>
</figure>
<p>A <em>unit</em> is a distinct type that also has arithmetic and a literal suffix:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">unit Seconds(s): i64
unit Millis(ms) = Seconds / 1000
unit Bytes(B): i64
unit KiB = 1024 B

let t = 2s + 500ms          // Millis 2500
let billed: Minutes = duration.ceiling()</code></pre>
<note>Planned: units are not in the compiler yet. A <code>unit</code> line is a syntax error today. The rules are these. Mixing units is an error. A value converts without a cast only toward a finer unit, as <code>2s + 500ms</code> does. Going to a coarser unit names its rounding with <code>t.floor&lt;Seconds&gt;()</code>, <code>t.ceiling&lt;Seconds&gt;()</code> or <code>t.rounded&lt;Seconds&gt;()</code> (BYN3205 if you leave it out). A bare literal never takes a unit, <code>0</code> included (BYN3203). Units are gone at run time. A unit prints as its value and symbol, as in <code>524288 B</code>. Counts such as <code>s.length</code> stay plain <code>i64</code>.</note>

<h2 id="collections">Arrays, spans and collections</h2>
<ul>
  <li><code>T[N]</code> is a fixed-size array stored inline. An index known at compile time that is out of range is an error (BYN2213).</li>
  <li><code>Span&lt;T&gt;</code> is a borrowed view of a run of elements. You can assign to the elements.</li>
  <li><code>ReadOnlySpan&lt;T&gt;</code> is a borrowed view you cannot assign through. A <code>Span&lt;T&gt;</code> converts to it. The other direction never converts.</li>
  <li><code>List&lt;T&gt;</code> and <code>Map&lt;K, V&gt;</code> own their elements and allocate. A <code>List</code> or an array becomes a span when you pass it as an argument. Elsewhere, <code>xs.span</code> gives a read-only view.</li>
  <li><code>xs[^1]</code> counts from the end. <code>xs[1..3]</code> is a span of the elements at positions 1 and 2.</li>
  <li><code>xs.remove(from: a, until: b)</code> removes the elements at positions <code>a</code> up to but not including <code>b</code> and keeps the order of the rest. With <code>through:</code> the end is included. A bad range stops the program with a panic.</li>
  <li><code>Map</code> keeps the order in which keys were inserted. It is the only hash map; <code>HashMap</code> is rejected with a fix (BYN2830).</li>
  <li>A <code>Map</code> key is an integer, a <code>rune</code>, a <code>bool</code>, a <code>string</code>, a plain enum, a <code>Symbol</code>, a tuple of keys, or a struct of keys. Any other key type is BYN2760, and a struct or tuple that is not a key is BYN2831.</li>
  <li>Hashing is seeded by default, to resist hash-flooding attacks.</li>
</ul>
<example name="ref-types-collections"/>
<pre><code class="byn">let scores: Map&lt;f64, i64&gt; = []</code></pre>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN2760:</span> `f64` cannot be a `Map` key
  --&gt; map-key.byn:1:17

<span class="gut">  1 │</span> let scores: Map&lt;f64, i64&gt; = []
<span class="gut">    │</span>                 <span class="hl">^^^</span>
<span class="gut">    │</span>
  help: use an integer type, `rune`, `bool`, `string`, `Symbol`, a plain enum, or a tuple or struct
        of those as the key</pre>
  <figcaption>The compiler’s message for this program.</figcaption>
</figure>
<pre><code class="byn">let grid: i32[3] = [1, 2, 3]
print($"{grid[7]}")</code></pre>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN2213:</span> index 7 is out of range for `i32[3]`
  --&gt; const-index.byn:2:15

<span class="gut">  2 │</span> print($"{grid[7]}")
<span class="gut">    │</span>               <span class="hl">^</span></pre>
  <figcaption>The compiler’s message for this program.</figcaption>
</figure>
<p>A <code>List</code>, a <code>Map</code> and a <code>StrBuf</code> own memory, so they are <code>unique</code>: assigning one moves it (see <a href="#unique">Move-only types</a>). A copy is written out, <code>xs.copy()</code>.</p>
<note>Planned: <code>Set&lt;T&gt;</code>, <code>Deque&lt;T&gt;</code>, <code>Pool&lt;T&gt;</code>, <code>Ring&lt;T, N&gt;</code>, <code>NonEmpty&lt;T&gt;</code> and <code>EnumMap</code> are in the specification, but the compiler reports <code>unknown type</code> for them. Typed indices (<code>List&lt;T, RowId&gt;</code> with <code>type distinct RowId = Index</code>), a module-level <code>const</code> array in read-only data and <code>StrBuf.over(stackalloc …)</code> are not in the compiler either.</note>

<h2 id="optionals">Optionals</h2>
<p><code>T?</code> is either a <code>T</code> or <code>null</code>. Plain types are never null.</p>
<ul>
  <li><code>x is null</code> and <code>x is not null</code> test it. After <code>is not null</code> on a <code>let</code> or a parameter, the value has its non-null type.</li>
  <li><code>x ?? fallback</code> gives the fallback when <code>x</code> is null.</li>
  <li><code>x ?? return v</code>, <code>x ?? break</code> and <code>x ?? continue</code> leave early, and <code>x ?? fail .e</code> fails. They also bind the non-null value: <code>let at = find(xs, at: 7) ?? return -1</code>.</li>
  <li><code>x == null</code> is rejected with a fix to <code>x is null</code> (BYN2027).</li>
</ul>
<example name="ref-types-optionals"/>
<note>Planned: the safe-navigation operator <code>?.</code> is in the specification but not in the compiler yet.</note>

<h2 id="tuples">Tuples</h2>
<p>A tuple groups values: <code>(1, "a")</code>. Elements may have names, and a name makes the element reachable with a dot: <code>(i64 count, string label)</code> as a type, <code>p.count</code> as an access. A tuple pattern takes a tuple apart in <code>switch</code>, in <code>is</code> and in the head of a <code>for</code> loop.</p>
<example name="ref-types-tuples"/>
<note>The compiler does not take a tuple apart in a <code>let</code> (<code>let (a, b) = t</code> is a syntax error), and it has no numeric access such as <code>t.0</code>. Name the elements, or use a pattern.</note>

<h2 id="results">Results</h2>
<p><code>Result&lt;T, E&gt;</code> holds either a value of type <code>T</code> or an error of type <code>E</code>, side by side in the same storage as a tag. A function that is declared <code>fails E</code> returns one under the hood. You can also name the type yourself. <code>r.isOk</code> tells which it is, <code>r.value</code> reads the value, <code>r.err</code> reads the error, and a value can be returned where a <code>Result</code> is expected. Formatting a <code>Result</code> prints its value or, for an error, its summary line. More in <a href="errors.html">Errors</a>.</p>
<example name="ref-types-result"/>

<h2 id="never">never</h2>
<p><code>never</code> is the type of an expression that does not come back: <code>panic</code>, <code>exit</code> and a loop with no exit. A <code>never</code> expression fits wherever a value is expected, in a <code>catch</code>, after <code>??</code> and in a <code>switch</code> arm. Code after one is unreachable.</p>
<example name="ref-types-never"/>

<h2 id="structs">Structs</h2>
<p>A struct is a value with named fields. Structs are the only data type with fields. There are no classes and no inheritance.</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Type first.</strong> A field, a parameter and a return type put the type before the name. A local binding puts the name first, <code>let w: Grams = 0g</code>, and <code>Grams w = 0g</code> inside a body is rejected with a fix (BYN1043).</li>
  <li><strong>Defaults.</strong> <code>i64 y = 0</code> gives a field a default. A struct literal may then leave it out. A type can be created with no values (is <em>defaultable</em>) only if every field has a default.</li>
  <li><strong>Computed members.</strong> <code>i64 sum =&gt; x + y</code> is a member that reads like a field. A member without arguments is a property. <code>sum</code> is read, not called.</li>
  <li><strong>Methods are read-only by default.</strong> A method that changes the struct has <code>ref this</code> as its first parameter. Without it the compiler reports BYN2115 and shows the fix. You cannot call a <code>ref this</code> method on a <code>let</code> (BYN2104).</li>
  <li><strong>Literals.</strong> <code>Point { x: 1 }</code> uses <code>:</code> for each field. A literal of a type with a <code>requires</code> rule is checked when it is built.</li>
  <li><strong>Equality</strong> compares field by field, and so does hashing. A struct is a <code>Map</code> key when all its fields are.</li>
  <li><strong>No default display.</strong> Putting a struct in a <code>$"{…}"</code> hole is an error unless it has a <code>formatInto</code> member or you write <code>{x, as: .debug}</code> (BYN2225).</li>
  <li><strong>Field writes.</strong> A struct with a <code>requires</code> rule, a <code>states</code> block or <code>@sealed</code> can be written only by its own <code>ref this</code> methods or through <code>with</code> (BYN3216). Plain data allows direct writes.</li>
</ul>
<example name="ref-types-struct"/>
<pre><code class="byn">struct Counter {
    i64 n
    void bump() {
        n += 1
    }
}</code></pre>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN2115:</span> `bump` modifies `this` but is not declared `ref this`
