<h3 id="constructors">Constructors and required fields</h3>
<p>A struct may declare a constructor, written as a function with the struct's name. Once it has one, you build the struct by calling it, and a literal is BYN2111. A <code>required</code> field must be set by the time construction ends.</p>
<example name="ref-types-constructor"/>

<h3 id="struct-planned">Embedding</h3>
<note>Planned: <code>@noEquality</code> (removes <code>==</code>) is rejected as an unknown attribute (BYN2770). <code>embed T</code>, which promotes the fields and methods of an embedded struct, is not in the compiler yet (BYN1004).</note>

<h2 id="unique">Move-only types</h2>
<p><code>struct unique</code> declares a type that cannot be copied. Its value has one owner, and it is cleaned up exactly once when that owner goes away. Use it for things like files and locks.</p>
<ul>
  <li>A type with a <code>unique</code> part is <code>unique</code> itself: a struct with a <code>List</code> field, a union with a <code>unique</code> payload, a tuple, array or optional of one. A struct holding a <code>Span&lt;T&gt;</code> (a mutable borrow) is <code>unique</code> too.</li>
  <li>A <code>unique</code> value is never copied by accident. <code>let g = f</code> is BYN2780, and the fix is <code>move f</code>, an explicit copy (<code>f.copy(to: alloc)</code>) or passing it by <code>ref</code>.</li>
  <li>A parameter of <code>unique</code> type takes <code>move</code> when the function takes ownership. A read-only parameter borrows.</li>
  <li>Once moved, the source cannot be used.</li>
  <li>A <code>unique</code> type cannot be <code>const</code> (BYN3209).</li>
</ul>
<example name="ref-types-unique"/>
<pre><code class="byn">struct unique File {
    i64 fd
}

let f = File { fd: 3 }
let g = f
print($"{g.fd}")</code></pre>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN2780:</span> implicit copy of a unique value: `File` is declared `unique struct`
  --&gt; implicit-copy.byn:6:9

<span class="gut">  6 │</span> let g = f
<span class="gut">    │</span>         <span class="hl">^</span>
<span class="gut">    │</span>
  help: hand the value over with `move f`; pass it by `ref` to share it instead
<span class="gut">    │</span> 6 │ let g = move f
<span class="gut">    │</span>   │         +++++</pre>
  <figcaption>The compiler’s message for this program.</figcaption>
</figure>
<p>The full ownership rules are in <a href="ref-memory.html">Memory model</a>.</p>

<h2 id="unions">Unions</h2>
<p>A union is a value that is exactly one of several cases. A case may carry named data. The storage is a tag plus room for the largest case, inline, with no allocation.</p>
<ul>
  <li>Case names are camelCase, one per line. A one-line union is rejected (BYN1053).</li>
  <li>Every payload is named in the declaration, <code>rect(i64 w, i64 h)</code>. A payload with no name is BYN1064.</li>
  <li>Construction may drop the label when the case has exactly one payload (<code>.percentOff(10)</code>). With several payloads, labels are required: <code>.rect(w: 3, h: 4)</code>.</li>
  <li>A leading dot, <code>.rect(…)</code>, names a case of the type the compiler expects at that place. <code>Shape.circle(2)</code> names it in full.</li>
  <li>A <code>switch</code> over a union must cover every case, and the error names the one you missed (BYN2410). A <code>_</code> arm hides later additions, so on a union from the same package it is a lint warning.</li>
  <li>Reading a payload is done by pattern: <code>.circle c</code> binds the whole case as <code>c</code>, and <code>.rect(let w, let h)</code> binds by position.</li>
  <li>A union is closed. There is no cast from an integer to a case. A payload-free case may be given an integer for talking to C, <code>read = 1</code>, which must be unique inside the union.</li>
</ul>
<example name="ref-types-union"/>
<pre><code class="byn">union Shape {
    circle(i64 r)
    empty
}

i64 area(Shape s) =&gt; s switch {
    .circle c =&gt; c.r,
}
print($"{area(.empty)}")</code></pre>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN2410:</span> `switch` does not handle every `Shape`
  --&gt; missing-case.byn:6:22

<span class="gut">  6 │</span> i64 area(Shape s) =&gt; s switch {
<span class="gut">    │</span>                      <span class="hl">^ not covered: .empty</span>
<span class="gut">    │</span>
  help: add the missing arms: .empty</pre>
  <figcaption>The compiler’s message for this program.</figcaption>
</figure>
<pre><code class="byn">union Shape {
    circle(i64)
    empty
}</code></pre>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN1064:</span> payload `i64` of `.circle` needs a name
  --&gt; payload-name.byn:2:12

<span class="gut">  2 │</span>     circle(i64)
<span class="gut">    │</span>            <span class="hl">^^^ no name</span>
<span class="gut">    │</span>
  help: name the payload: `.circle(i64 name)`</pre>
