<h2 id="enums">Enums</h2>
<p>An enum is a closed set of named values, each with the same constant data. The enum value is a small number. Its data lives in read-only tables.</p>
<ul>
  <li>A value's data is listed in the declaration: <code>mercury(3, 24)</code> for the header <code>enum Planet(i64 mass, i64 radius)</code>. The data must be constant.</li>
  <li>The compiler generates <code>name</code>, <code>ordinal</code>, <code>values</code>, <code>count</code>, <code>parse(text)</code> and <code>fromOrdinal(n)</code>. The last two return an optional. Today the member is <code>count</code>, where the specification says <code>length</code>.</li>
  <li>There is no cast from an integer to an enum. <code>fromOrdinal</code> is the checked way.</li>
  <li>An enum <code>switch</code> must cover every value, like a union.</li>
</ul>
<example name="ref-types-enum"/>
<note>Planned: members on an enum (<code>public f64 surfaceGravity =&gt; …</code> after the values) are a syntax error today. <code>enum open</code> (unknown values, for C interop) and <code>extensible</code> (callers must supply a fallback case) are not in the compiler yet.</note>

<h2 id="error-sets">Error sets</h2>
<p><code>union error</code> declares a set of failures, with or without data. It shares the union grammar. The difference is where it may be used: in <code>fails</code> clauses and in <code>catch switch</code>. The behavior of <code>fails</code>, <code>try</code> and <code>catch</code> is in <a href="ref-expressions.html#catch">Expressions</a> and <a href="errors.html">Errors</a>.</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Compose</strong> sets with <code>=</code> and commas: <code>union error ShipError = CarrierError, StockError</code>. The result has all the cases of its parts, and a part converts to the whole implicitly.</li>
  <li><strong>Narrow</strong> a set with <code>except</code>: <code>union error Retryable = CarrierError except .rejected</code>.</li>
  <li>Two parts that share a case name are an error (BYN3202).</li>
  <li>A case name belongs to its set. <code>EmailError.invalid</code> and <code>UrlError.invalid</code> are different, and an ambiguous <code>.invalid</code> is an error.</li>
  <li>A doc comment on a case is its message. <code>$"{e}"</code> prints the first line (see <a href="ref-lexical.html#doc-comments">Doc comments</a>).</li>
  <li><code>IoError</code> and <code>Utf8Error</code> are built in.</li>
</ul>
<example name="ref-types-errors"/>

<h2 id="interfaces">Interfaces</h2>
<p>An interface is a set of members a type promises to have.</p>
<ul>
  <li>Conformance is explicit: <code>struct Square : IShape</code>. Interface names begin with <code>I</code>.</li>
  <li>A member with a body is a default method. Each conforming type gets its own copy.</li>
  <li>A property requirement is a bare <code>Type name</code>. A requirement that changes the receiver has <code>ref this</code>, and so must every implementation.</li>
  <li>A method's <code>uses</code> clause in an interface is an upper bound. An implementation may use fewer effects.</li>
  <li>Used as a generic constraint (<code>where T : IShape</code>), the call is resolved when the program is built, with no cost at run time.</li>
  <li>Used as <code>dyn ref IShape</code>, the call goes through a table. A <code>dyn ref</code> parameter needs a <code>var</code> argument, and <code>dyn ref readonly I</code> allows a <code>let</code> but only members without <code>ref this</code> (BYN2813). An interface name alone as a type is BYN2816.</li>
  <li>Only structs conform (BYN2815 for unions and enums).</li>
</ul>
<example name="ref-types-interface"/>
<note>Planned: retroactive conformance (<code>extension Money : IJsonEncode</code>), <code>static abstract</code> members and generic interface methods are not in the compiler yet.</note>

<h2 id="function-types">Function types</h2>
<p><code>A -&gt; B</code> is the type of a function from <code>A</code> to <code>B</code>. Write <code>(A, B) -&gt; C</code> for two parameters and <code>() -&gt; void</code> for none. After the return type, <code>fails E</code> and then <code>uses e</code> add error sets and effects. <code>-&gt;</code> is for types. <code>=&gt;</code> is for lambdas and bodies.</p>
<ul>
  <li>A function type before a name needs parentheses: <code>by (i64 -&gt; i64) f</code>. An alias avoids that: <code>type Pred = i64 -&gt; bool</code>, then <code>by Pred p</code>.</li>
  <li>Two function types are equal when the parameters, return type, error sets and effects are equal. There is no variance.</li>
  <li>A <em>named function</em> works as a value when it is a non-generic, module-level function whose signature matches exactly. Particles are part of the name, not the type. A mismatch is BYN2202, and a method or generic function used as a value is BYN2824.</li>
  <li>A <em>lambda</em> is <code>x =&gt; …</code>, <code>(a, b) =&gt; …</code> or <code>() =&gt; …</code>. Its parameters are bare names. Their types come from the type expected at that place.</li>
  <li><code>it</code>: an argument that mentions <code>it</code>, where a one-parameter function is expected, is a lambda with the parameter <code>it</code>.</li>
  <li>A lambda copies the <code>let</code> values it reads when it is created. It cannot capture a <code>var</code> (BYN2821) or a <code>unique</code> value.</li>
  <li>A function value is a borrow of the frame that made it. It may be passed down but not stored or returned.</li>
  <li>A function type is allowed as a parameter, an alias target and a local. As a field it is BYN2822.</li>
</ul>
<example name="ref-types-function"/>
<pre><code class="byn">type Pricer = i64 -&gt; i64

struct Shop {
    Pricer pricer
}</code></pre>
