<h2 id="states">Typestate</h2>
<p>A struct can declare the states it passes through. The state becomes part of its type, so using a value in the wrong state is a compile error that costs nothing at run time.</p>
<ul>
  <li>States are declared in one <code>states { … }</code> block after the fields, one per line, each with optional named payload. At most one block per struct.</li>
  <li><code>Order.paid</code> is the type of an <code>Order</code> known to be in state <code>paid</code>. It is valid anywhere a type is. <code>Order</code> alone means any state.</li>
  <li>A literal names its state, <code>Order.draft { id: 7 }</code>. A plain <code>Order { … }</code> is BYN7005.</li>
  <li>The receiver's state is written <code>this is .paid</code>, <code>ref this is .draft</code>, or with <code>or</code> for several: <code>this is .paid or .shipped</code>.</li>
  <li>A payload is readable only when the type fixes the state (BYN7002). With several states, a field is readable if each has it with the same type.</li>
  <li><code>becomes .paid</code> after the parameter list declares a transition, and <code>become .paid(amount)</code> performs it. Only a <code>ref this</code> method can declare it. It must <code>become</code> on every path that returns normally. A failed call leaves the state unchanged.</li>
  <li>After a <code>becomes</code> call on a <code>var</code>, the variable has the new state in the rest of the scope.</li>
  <li>Widening is free: <code>Order.paid</code> goes where <code>Order</code> is expected. Narrowing is done with <code>is</code> or <code>switch</code>.</li>
  <li>A <code>switch</code> over the states must cover each one, so a new state is an error at every switch that misses it (BYN7004).</li>
  <li>Layout is the same as the plain struct: a tag plus a payload. States do not exist at run time.</li>
</ul>
<example name="ref-types-typestate"/>
<pre><code class="byn">struct Order {
    i64 id
    states {
        draft
        paid(i64 payment)
    }
    void pay(ref this is .draft, i64 amount) becomes .paid {
        become .paid(amount)
    }
}

var o = Order.draft { id: 1 }
o.pay(5)
o.pay(5)</code></pre>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN7001:</span> `pay` needs `o` to be `.draft`, but it is `Order.paid`
